Ladybird’s HTML parser now keeps its speculative parser from one
blocking script to the next, and its preload scanner goes on from the
end of the input it has already scanned. When document.write() inserts
input ahead of that point, or the parser gets past it, the next scan
starts over at the parser’s position. Blink’s HTMLDocumentParser
also keeps one preload_scanner_ fed through AppendToEnd(), and WebKit’s
HTMLDocumentParser::append() feeds its m_preloadScanner the same way,
dropping it once the parser has caught up with the input. Both scan
document.write() input with a separate insertion preload scanner
instead (insertion_preload_scanner_, m_insertionPreloadScanner), rather
than starting over as Ladybird does. That’s from reading their source.
